Reopen on duplicate content instead of clobbering

new_task today calls object::create which uses the content blob's SHA as the stable id. If a user pushes a task whose content matches an existing (typically dropped) task, the create succeeds silently and overwrites the namespace binding for that stable id, effectively losing the old binding's history pointer.

Detect the collision: before create, check whether refs/tsk/tasks/<sha> already exists.
- If it does and the task is currently bound in the active namespace with status=done: flip status back to open, push to the active queue, return the existing human id (i.e. 'reopen').
- If it does and is bound elsewhere: error, suggest 'tsk share' or explicit 'tsk reopen -T tsk-N'.
- If it does and is unbound in the active namespace: bind it (assign a fresh human id) and push.

This makes pushing a duplicate body the natural way to revive a finished task — no separate command needed. The dedicated 'tsk reopen' (tsk-14) becomes a convenience wrapper that takes an explicit task id.